course: dictionary
lesson: 953
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Bloomer is one of them, so the context, meaning the words around it, tells you which meaning is right. Ask yourself: which meaning fits this sentence?
explain: «Many late bloomers catch up» tells you a late bloomer is someone who grows or develops later than others. The clue is catch up: they start behind, but then they reach the same level.
explain: In everyday British talk, «I made a right bloomer» means you made a silly mistake. In a bakery, a bloomer is a large loaf of bread. Swap your chosen meaning into the sentence to check it still makes sense.
fact: Bloomer has several meanings, so always check the context.
fact: «Bloomer» (UK, informal) means a silly mistake.
fact: «Bloomer» can also mean a large loaf of white bread with diagonal cuts on top.
fact: «Late bloomer» means a child or person who develops or succeeds later than others.
fact: «Bloomers» (plural) means long, loose knickers or trousers once worn by women and girls, now old-fashioned or joking.
exercise: Jess said, «I made a right bloomer when I dropped the cake.» What does bloomer mean here? | A pair of old-fashioned trousers | *A silly mistake | A plant that flowers | A large loaf of bread
exercise: The baker sold a big bloomer with diagonal cuts on top. What is a bloomer here? | A silly mistake | A kind of garden flower | A late bloomer | *A large loaf of white bread
exercise: «Many late bloomers catch up.» What does late bloomer mean? | Someone who bakes bread | Someone who loves flowers | Someone who is always first in the race | *Someone who develops later than others
test: In a story set long ago, the girl wore bloomers to the picnic. What are bloomers here? | *Long, loose knickers or trousers worn long ago | A large loaf of bread | A flower in a pot | A silly mistake
test: Sam is a late bloomer, so he reads more slowly for now. What does this suggest? | Sam is the fastest reader in the class | Sam is a baker | Sam likes flowers | *Sam is behind for now but may catch up
exam: Which phrase means a child who is behind other children for now? | bloomers | make a bloomer | *late bloomer | a bloomer loaf
